{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Feedback Versus Input.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Feedback Versus Input.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"feedback versus input feedback versus input","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Feedback Versus Input.md","fileName":"Feedback Versus Input.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Feedback Versus Input","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-21 00:27\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Game Playtesting Practice]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Playtesting Practice","gamesdesignandplay.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Feedback Versus Input.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Feedback Versus Input.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"feedback versus input feedback versus input","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Feedback Versus Input.md","fileName":"Feedback Versus Input.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Feedback Versus Input","heading":"Feedback Versus Input","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Playtest feedback describes where the game did or did not produce the intended experience. Input proposes a change to the work. Both can be useful, but they carry different authority.\n\nThe designer owns the diagnosis and solution because a tester's suggestion may solve a different problem or contradict the project's values. Separating the observed shortfall from the proposed feature lets the team consider several remedies without dismissing the underlying evidence.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Playtesting Practice","gamesdesignandplay.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Feedback Versus Input.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Feedback Versus Input.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"feedback versus input feedback versus input","f